          "text": "INTERACTIVE PROOF - ELEMENTARY NUMBER THEORY\n\nWhy Odd Numbers Build Perfect Squares\n\nTHEOREM\n\nFor every natural number n, the sum of the first n odd numbers is n2: 1 + 3 + 5 + ... + (2n - 1) = n2.\n\nPROOF BY INDUCTION\n\nWrite Sn for 1 + 3 + ... + (2n - 1). For n = 0, the sum is empty, so S0 = 0 = 02. Now assume Sn = n2. The next odd number is 2n + 1, hence\n\nSn+1 = Sn + (2n + 1) = n2 + 2n + 1 = (n + 1)2.\n\nTherefore the identity holds for n + 1, and induction proves it for every natural number n.\n\nWHY THE ALGEBRA MATCHES THE PICTURE\n\nTo enlarge an n by n square, attach one column of n cells and one row of n + 1 cells. The new L-shaped border contains 2n + 1 cells - exactly the next odd number. The equation (n + 1)2 - n2 = 2n + 1 is the same growth step written algebraically.\n\nFORMALIZATION NOTE\n\nLean defines oddSum recursively: oddSum 0 = 0 and oddSum (n + 1) = oddSum n + (2 * n + 1). The theorem follows the same induction as the paper; simplification expands the successor square and closes the arithmetic step.\n\nCopyright 2026 Interactive Proof Project. Licensed CC BY 4.0 - creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
